## Branch Strategy

> **Rewritten 03.08.2026.** The previous scheme (a branch per feature, PR into `dev`, PR into
> `master`) was ceremony without its benefit: branches isolate parallel work, but this project runs
> several sessions **against one working tree**, so every session writes to the same branch anyway.
> `feat/slice-feel-and-content` lived three weeks and collected 789 commits — a second `main` under a
> different name. See journal `2026-08-03-speed-comes-from-the-local-build-not-from-skipping-checks`.

### Structure

```
master   ← the shop window: what gets shown to people. Updated by PR from dev, before a demo.
dev      ← where the work happens. Commit here directly.
<topic>  ← only for a risky run you would not mind abandoning (a rewrite, an experiment)
```

### Rules

- **Commit to `dev` directly.** No branch per feature, per fix, or per content change.
- **A branch is for work that may not survive** — rewriting combat, trying a renderer. Not for
  "a feature". If you would merge it no matter what, it belongs on `dev`.
- **`master` moves only by PR from `dev`**, and only when something is being shown. It is not part of
  the fix-and-play loop.
- **NEVER squash, NEVER rebase-merge — every commit is kept** (Max, 03.08.2026: «Все коммиты должны
  сохраняться»). Squash rewrites N commits into one new hash, the target branch stops seeing the
  originals as ancestors, and the next release PR opens in conflict — that is exactly how `master` and
  `dev` split in July and produced a 419-file conflict two weeks later. Both options are now **disabled
  in the repository settings**, so the button cannot offer them.
- **Do not delete a merged branch** until its history is confirmed in the target branch.
- No broken compilation in any commit.
- **Commit per stage:** one commit per completed stage of a multi-stage feature, each compiling and
  green. Every stage is then a rollback point and the history reads as the plan's steps.
- `dev` may go red — you are the one who fixes it. CI on a push costs no waiting: it runs while you
  work, and it exists for one thing local runs cannot do — catching what never reached the repository.
- `CI Gate` is the required status check on `dev` and `master`.

### Before pushing

Local runs are faster than CI and catch the same code problems:

```powershell
./scripts/compile-check.ps1                      # seconds
./scripts/run-tests.ps1 -Mode EditMode -Where Shadow   # minutes
```

What they cannot catch is a file you forgot to commit — that is CI's job, and the reason it stays.

